From 53c07f753cbdaa5b09f82497b5c5e0e7c86c098c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 17:02:46 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(tools): wait_hint on a foreground sleep that had a
 process or card to wait on [daveey]

A foreground terminal command that sleeps a minute or more, or poll-loops with
sleeps of 5 s or more, gets a `wait_hint` field when the caller's task has a
tracked running job (names process_manage wait) or the command reads one
card's status (worker: park with awaiting kind=card; chat: the completion
notice). Measured on the hub over 7 days: half of 52 h of sleep-polling sat
behind a tracked process and 28% re-read another card.

Contract (tools/terminal_tool_wait_hint.py):
- Only adds a field. Nothing is refused or rewritten; the scan is one linear
  pass with an 8,000-character bound.
- Only for a foreground command whose own execution took 30 s or more (the
  successful env.execute attempt, timed in _run_foreground: not the text, and
  not the approval wait, sandbox start or retries) and finished cleanly:
  exit 0, no error, no status, not backgrounded by the tool. A non-zero exit is
  also how a timeout, an interrupt and some backend failures report, so those
  get nothing.
- The process hint carries timeout=<the measured time>, so following it never
  blocks longer than the command it replaces, even if the process is a server.
- Silence over a wrong hint: none when the command then looks elsewhere
  (service probe, CI, remote host), for a recognised server or notify-on-exit
  process, or for a worker's own card. Quotes, expansions, heredoc bodies and
  comments are not read as commands by the sleep scan.

Known imprecision: the board-database card check reads the raw command, and
neither card check looks at whether the read came after the sleep.

Not covered: a chat waiting on a card it did not create has no subscribe path.
The process hint matches on the calling turn's task id, so a process started in
an earlier one-shot turn is not seen.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
